message.content:
Yes – you can attach a comment directly to an IP‑address record in VirusTotal via its **v3 REST API**.  
Below is everything you’ll need to make the request, plus two quick “how‑to” snippets (curl & Python) so you can copy‑paste and run it yourself.

---

### What you’re sending

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| `POST` URL | `https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/comments` |
| Header – API Key | `X‑Apikey: <YOUR_API_KEY>` |
| Header – Content‑Type | `application/json` |
| Body | A single *Comment* resource whose `relationships.target.data.id`
is the IP (`10.0.0.1`) and whose `relationships.target.data.type`
is `"ipv4_addresses"` |

```json
{
  "data": {
    "type": "comment",
    "attributes": {
      "text": "Needs review #unsure"
    },
    "relationships": {
      "target": {
        "data": {
          "id": "10.0.0.1",
          "type": "ipv4_addresses"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

That will create a comment attached to the IP‑address entry for `10.0.0.1`.

---

### Quick reference

#### 1️⃣ Using `curl`

```bash
curl -s \
     --request POST "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/comments" \
     --header "x-apikey: YOUR_VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY_HERE" \
     --header "content-type: application/json" \
     --data @- <<EOF
{
  "data": {
    "type": "comment",
    "attributes": {
      "text": "Needs review #unsure"
    },
    "relationships": {
      "target": {
        "data": {
          "id": "10.0.0.1",
          "type": "ipv4_addresses"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
EOF
```

Replace `YOUR_VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY_HERE` with the key you received from VirusTotal.

#### 2️⃣ Using Python (`requests`)

```python
import requests
import json

api_key = "YOUR_VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY_HERE"

url = "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/comments"
headers = {
    "x-apikey": api_key,
    "content-type": "application/json"
}

payload = {
    "data": {
        "type": "comment",
        "attributes": {
            "text": "Needs review #unsure"
        },
        "relationships": {
            "target": {
                "data": {
                    "id": "10.0.01",
                    "type": "ipv4_addresses"
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

response = requests.post(url, headers=headers, data=json.dumps(payload))

print("Status:", response.status_code)
print("Response:", response.json())
```

Again, swap out the placeholder API key before running.

---

### Things to keep in mind

| Tip | Why it matters |
|-----|----------------|
| **Rate limits** | VirusTotal enforces per‑minute quotas. Keep track of your usage if you plan many calls. |
| **Permissions** | Your API key must belong to a subscription level that allows writing comments (usually the free tier does). |
| **Error handling** | A non‑200 status means either bad auth, malformed payload, or quota exceeded. Inspect `response.text`. |
| **Security** | Never commit your raw API key to public repos. Store it securely (env vars, secret managers, etc.). |

If you hit any errors, let me know the exact HTTP status code and error message—happy to troubleshoot!

